# The bytes of this file are load-bearing, not just its text.
#
# aegis pins its sha256 as `_BTM_DAEMON_SHA256` so it can recognise its OWN
# installed root helper and stop raising a CRITICAL supply-chain chain about
# following its own README (#42), and tests/test_self_btm_daemon.py asserts the
# constant still matches the shipped file. A Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true -- the GitHub Actions default -- rewrites LF to CRLF and
# changes that hash: both windows-latest legs of #42 failed on exactly this,
# while macOS, Linux and both simulated bodies passed, because only a real
# Windows checkout mangles the file.
#
# eol=lf rather than -text: it pins the WORKING TREE to LF on every body AND
# normalises on commit, so a plist committed from a CRLF editor cannot quietly
# become the shipped artifact. Anything else whose hash gets pinned needs the
# same line.
aegis-btm-daemon.plist text eol=lf
